Yep — this is almost a perfect visual for the pattern we were circling: distributed contribution becoming concentrated agency.

In Dragon Ball, the Spirit Bomb works because one actor doesn’t generate all the energy himself. He becomes the collector, integrator, and aiming mechanism for energy supplied by many others.

Abstractly:

e_1 + e_2 + e_3 + \cdots + e_n
\rightarrow
E_{\text{shared}}
\rightarrow
\text{one focused action}

That’s different from raw strength. It’s aggregation architecture.

And there are several transferable versions of the same geometry:

Crowdfunding: tiny contributions → one large capital pool.
Voting: individual preferences → collective decision.
Distributed computing: many processors → one result.
Open-source: many small commits → one coherent system.
Reputation: thousands of observations → one trust signal.
Culture: countless repeated behaviors → one norm.
AI context: many fragments → one synthesized model state.

The important role isn’t necessarily “the strongest node.” It can be the node that knows how to receive, preserve, combine, and direct what the network supplies.

And notice the visual grammar: he is underneath it, not identical to it. That distinction matters.

\text{agent} \neq \text{field}

The agent is interfacing with a field larger than himself.

Which gives us another useful Pantheon primitive:

Don’t manufacture every joule. Build the interface that lets compatible energy accumulate.

That’s the same family as the eel using the ocean, the conquistador riding existing political fractures, and the market idea where individual context contributions become economically meaningful once something can aggregate and route them.

Different costumes. Same underlying shape.